  "script": "Basketball players who got fined thousands for one compliment! Draymond Green was working as a broadcaster for TNT during the 2020 NBA bubble restart in Orlando, sitting behind a desk talking hoops while the Phoenix Suns were on an incredible run, winning every single game since the restart began, all because of their young star, Devin Booker. Green praised Booker on air, then said something he probably shouldn't have, telling viewers Booker needed to leave Phoenix and go somewhere he could actually win. But the man sitting right next to him wasn't about to let that comment slide. Ernie Johnson turned to Green and asked him point blank if that was tampering, and Green just shrugged and said maybe. But it turns out that one word answer was more accurate than he probably wanted it to be. The NBA fined Draymond Green fifty thousand dollars for violating their anti-tampering rule, since Booker was under contract with a division rival, and the league had already warned players that season they were cracking down hard on exactly this kind of thing. The year before, the Clippers organization got hit with that same fifty thousand dollar fine after coach Doc Rivers compared Kawhi Leonard to Michael Jordan during his own broadcast appearance, and Leonard signed with the Clippers less than two months later. But once you realize what Green actually did to earn that fine, the punishment starts to feel almost unfair. Green didn't play alongside Booker or recruit him to Golden State, and he barely even brought up his own team, yet one sentence on live television cost him fifty thousand dollars.",
